  "prompt": "[system]\n\n    You generate compact, robust Python TSP metaheuristics.\n    You must follow the requested interface exactly, always preserve tour validity.\n    \n\n[user]\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000004: lk_improver\n\nStatus: valid; distance: 17.5813\n\nThought: Start from nearest neighbor constructive tour, then iteratively apply 2-opt edge exchanges (a basic Lin-Kernighan-style variable-depth search limited to depth 1) to improve the tour. Report new best tours as soon as found.\n\nCode:\n\n```python\n\nimport numpy as np\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix):\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    # nearest neighbor construction\n    tour = [0]\n    unvisited = set(range(1,n))\n    curr = 0\n    while unvisited:\n        nxt = min(unvisited, key=lambda c: distance_matrix[curr,c])\n        tour.append(nxt)\n        unvisited.remove(nxt)\n        curr = nxt\n    tour = np.array(tour, dtype=np.int32)\n    best_tour = tour.copy()\n    best_cost = 0.0\n    for i in range(n):\n        best_cost += distance_matrix[tour[i], tour[(i+1)%n]]\n    report_best_tour(best_tour)\n    improved = True\n    while improved:\n        improved = False\n        # 2-opt improvement\n        for i in range(n):\n            for j in range(i+2, n):\n                a,b = tour[i], tour[(i+1)%n]\n                c,d = tour[j], tour[(j+1)%n]\n                if distance_matrix[a,c] + distance_matrix[b,d] < distance_matrix[a,b] + distance_matrix[c,d]:\n                    # reverse segment from i+1 to j\n                    tour[i+1:j+1] = tour[i+1:j+1][::-1]\n                    cost = 0.0\n                    for k in range(n):\n                        cost += distance_matrix[tour[k], tour[(k+1)%n]]\n                    if cost < best_cost:\n                        best_cost = cost\n                        best_tour = tour.copy()\n                        report_best_tour(best_tour)\n                    improved = True\n                    break\n            if improved:\n                break\n    return best_tour\n\n```\n\nVERBAL GRADIENT: follow the reflection below as the primary source of change.\n\nUse the supplied parents as supporting evidence only.\n\nPARENT-SPECIFIC LLM REFLECTIONS:\n\nParent cand_000004 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: Nearest neighbor plus single-move 2-opt yields valid tours with moderate quality and no timeouts, but local search is limited.\n- Aim: Improve tour quality by enabling full 2-opt scanning and adding restarts with random perturbation.\n- Preserve: nearest neighbor construction; valid permutation guarantee; timeout robustness\n- Change: replace single-move 2-opt with full-scan 2-opt until local minimum; add outer restart loop with randomized initial tours or perturbation\n- Avoid: breaking valid permutation; exceeding timeout; introducing runtime errors\n\nDistance is the search objective for TSP; lower distance is better.\n\nImplement a complete TSP metaheuristic inside this required entrypoint:\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n\nRequirements:\n- Minimize total TSP tour distance.\n- Return a 1D np.ndarray containing each node exactly once.\n- Do not repeat the start node at the end.\n- Always return a valid tour.\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates.\n- Create a valid tour early.\n- Call report_best_tour(tour) whenever a new best valid tour is found, assume this func already exists.\n- Do not use files, network, subprocesses, or external solvers.\n- Keep the code compact and robust.\n\ncorrect signature, valid tour on every return path,\nearly report_best_tour, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short_snake_case_name\",\n  \"thought\": \"high-level summary of the construction, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_tsp.",
